In article <1150236178.056582.260510@p79g2000cwp.googlegroups.com>, 
dfreybur@yahoo.com says...
> Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> > More directly, the unconvertable altars aren't the
> > unaligned ones, but the Gehennom ones (which happen
> > to be unaligned).
> Ah.  Good point.
> > Which, I suppose, raises a source-diver's query. I
> > already (think I) know that the occasionaly randomly
> > generated _attended_ altar can exist. Can randomly
> > generated altars (attended or not) be unaligned?
> Near as I can tell any shrine in the main dungeon will
> have an alignment and so it will be convertable.

Earlier in this thread, even in the very part you quoted above (see 
above your 'Good point.'), it has been well established that 
'unalignedness' has nothing to do with 'convertability'.

Therefore, to repeat this one more time, even *if* (through some freak 
bug etc) there would be an unaligned altar in the main dungeon it would 
be convertable. Repeating again, the 'unconvertability' is due to the 
altar's location, specifically so that any altar located in Gehennom 
(including VotD and sanctum) will not be convertable, and any other 
altar will be, no matter it's (un)alignedness.

Perhaps this rule could even be simplified to say that "all altars 
except in not-safe-to-pray areas can be converted, the others can 
never". What does a sourcediver say, is the code checking for the 
location directly by itself, or is it dependent on the safe-to-pray 
check?

> Same
> with the one in the mines.  The one in the valley of the
> dead is in Gehennom, explicitly unaligned, attended
> by a priest and I don't think convertable. 

Again, if you read the part you quoted above 'Good point.', this is 
already made clear, and also that it's not because of it's 
unalignedness.

> The one or
> more deeper in Gehennom aren't attended that I've ever
> seen but not convertable.
> That leaves the quests.  I haven't been through enough
> of them to know.  I think those are all convertable but I
> have no idea if any are attended.

Convertable, because of the same rule.


This misconception definitely needs to go in the 'common myths' 
collection. :)

Perhaps something like this...

In article <MPG.1ef7ab2947c0648798a515@198.186.190.61>, rast2
@hotmail.com says...
> MYTH: Unaligned altars can't be converted; it is harmful to even try.

FACT: If an altar is convertable or not is not in any way dependent on 
if it is aligned or unaligned. The only altars that cannot be converted 
are those in Gehennom (including VotD and Sanctum).

...or replace that last line with:

"are those on levels where it is not safe to pray, see Matthew Lahut's 
Praying Spoiler[link]."

...if that matches the workings of the source code better. This to make 
the information more futureproof and general, and to give the reader the 
actual (decodeified) Nethack logic of the matter.


I see that Eva has already updated the myths page though:
"MYTH: Unaligned altars (altars to Moloch) can't be converted; it is 
harmful to even try.
FACT: Unaligned altars in Gehennom cannot be converted. Unaligned altars 
outside of Gehennom, such as those on some quests, can be converted 
normally. It is, however, wholly impossible to convert oneself to 
Moloch. "

